Z||000000||MotherGoose||06-07-2000||04:46 PM||||Got my 87GT to start yesterday.  It sounded better than it ever has.  So I let it idle a while, then I jump in for a spin.  Turn out of the driveway, give it some gas and, it dies.  Won't restart.  <P>It has new plugs and wires, fuel filter, and has gas in it.  I know the gas is getting to the engine because I check the schader valve.  <P>It didn't sound like it ran out of gas,  it sound like it just quit, no sputter or nothing, just died.  The fuses are Ok, and the computer doesn't show and codes just 12, 12, 12.<P>Please help or I'm going to get the 45 and put her out of my misery. ||||reg||8
Z||000001||batboy||06-07-2000||05:11 PM||||You say it's getting gas, but is it getting spark?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||06-07-2000||05:15 PM||||<BR>How did the engine act when you tried re-starting the engine?  Did it click? Did it turn over?<P>Depending on the reaction of the engine, it could be something easy (like a poor electrical connection at the battery) or a bigger situation (like a broken timing chain).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||MotherGoose||06-07-2000||05:23 PM||||It turns like normal.  Nothing odd, the damn thing just won't start again.  Does an ignition coil just go out like that?  If so that could be it, thats one that hasn't been replaced.<P>thanks<P>Goose||||reg||8
Z||000004||cscougar||06-07-2000||05:31 PM||||I had one do that and it was the neutral safety switch.  If it goes out then you get lights and all but no power to the starter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||cscougar||06-07-2000||05:31 PM||||I had one do that and it was the neutral safety switch.  If it goes out then you get lights and all but no power to the starter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||sluppy123||06-07-2000||05:37 PM||||Check your ignition module, when they go bad the car just dies, plus it's a fairly common problem on the v-6's. <P>-Brian<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||06-07-2000||05:42 PM||||Yup what he said, and when the coil or module cools down, they work again. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||MotherGoose||06-07-2000||05:44 PM||||It has a brand new ignition module.  And the coil shouldn't have been that hot.  will the coil just go out?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula||06-07-2000||06:36 PM||||my neutral start switch did that too and I just had to plug it back in after I cleaned it good.||||reg||1
Z||000010||William Federle||06-07-2000||07:17 PM||||Now don't laugh, but I had a 1981 or so Renault Le Car. It had electronic ignition with a distributor. If it was cold, it would start right up. One time I backed it out of the garage and parked it in the sun in the driveway. 1/2 hour later it wouldn't start. If I drove it after starting it, it would die right on the road and I'd coast to the side and let it cool off. It turned out to be a heat-sensitive module in the distributor-the thing that a rotating component would activate this module to trigger the coil. After that was replaced the problem disappeared. Maybe your problem is similar.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||BAM-BAM||06-07-2000||07:33 PM||||  Goose, there's a couple things I would try if I were you. First, did you use the heat sinking compound when you replaced your ignition module and second how are the cap and rotor, were the plugs gapped properly, were they the right plugs, and how new or old  is the coil? Don't be mad at the car, it is impossible for a car to be stupid, rather the cars owner might just be uneducated about certain aspects of the vehical. That's why we have this forum. Hell, who knows what I would have done without this place. Be patient and ask lots and lots of questions. Believe me, there isn't a problem that you can have(with your Fiero) that someone on this forum hasn't also experienced and fixed. It's obviously something with your ignition electronics if you have good fuel pressure and the motor turns over. Worst case, replace everything to do with the ignition electronics. Also, check your ECM for error codes.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000012||1FST2M6||06-07-2000||08:13 PM||||how many miles are on the car?... ever think of the Timing Chain breaking?!?  it would still sound the same but not start or run... ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Homicidal||06-07-2000||08:26 PM||||I think he said he already pulled codes.<P>Could it be the ICM AGAIN?  Get a Voltometer and check the coil and ICM again.  Maybe it blew again!  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||06-07-2000||09:49 PM||||Sounds like the ingnition pick-up coil. Check for approximatly 1200 ohms. If not, you'll have to pull you distributor and take it apart and replace the coil. It's about $5-7. Cheap fix.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000015||MotherGoose||06-08-2000||10:39 AM||||Ok here is the latest.<P>I have great spark, nice and blue.  Fuses are good, brand new fuel filter, and I here the pump running when I try to kick it over.  But I checked the pressure last night and it was barely a trickle.  I think I'm going to drop the tank new lines, filter, and pump.  How hard is this?  Any suggestions?<P>Thanks Everyone ||||reg||4
Z||000016||Big O||06-08-2000||11:52 AM||||Dropping the tank and changing the fuel pump, lines and harness is a snap.  Be sure the tank is almost empty before you do it and make sure your garage is well ventilated if thats where your going to do it.  I dropped mine twice to work on the fuel pump.  It only takes 15-30 minutes.||||reg||1
